 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Requires courts, prior to accepting a plea, to provide notice to the defendant that such plea and the acceptance thereof could result in deportation, removal from the United States, exclusion from the United States or denial of citizenship, if the defendant is not a citizen of the United States.",
 "title": "Requires notice of risk of deportation of non-citizens, prior to accepting a plea",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A3057",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A3057-2023",
     "printNo": "A3057",
     "session": 2023,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 117\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 3057, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to requir-\n      ing the court, prior to accepting a plea, to advise the  defendant\n      of the risk of deportation if he or she is not a citizen\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  The  purpose  of  this  bill  would be to notify defendants during the\nmisdemeanor and violation plea process that pleading guilty may  subject\nthe defendant to automatic deportation or denial of naturalization.\n \n  I  support  the laudable goal of strengthening due process protections\nfor all. Due process protections are currently in place for  noncitizens\ntaking pleas and defense attorneys are already required by law to advise\ntheir clients of the immigration consequences of any conviction.\n \n  This  bill  would  impose a rigid requirement that the court deliver a\nspecific warning about potential immigration consequences in all  cases.\nIt would also prohibit a court from mentioning anything else about immi-\ngration  consequences  that  might  be relevant to the specific case and\nwould prohibit the prosecutor from mentioning  immigration  consequences\nat  all  --  while  requiring  the plea to be vacated if those technical\nprovisions were not strictly complied with. Finally, it includes  retro-\nactive  components  that  would  automatically  vacate certain pleas for\nviolation of provisions that did not exist at  the  time  the  plea  was\ntaken.\n \n  The  bill  is  duplicative of existing due process protections and the\nhyper-technical requirements of this legislation  would  result  in  the\nvacatur  of  otherwise  lawful  convictions  where defendants were fully\naware of the immigration consequences at the time the plea was taken.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 117,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2023
   }
  ],
  "size": 1
 },
